#### Pointer, projection and target-resolution contract

1. Capture one immutable perspective-frame context at Opaque order 0.49, before the existing box's 0.51 draw: copies of PerspectiveViewMat/PerspectiveProjectionMat, CameraMatrixOriginf, encoded CameraPos, active dimension, actual camera mode and viewport size. The projection supplies the camera far plane; player PickingRange does not limit any gizmo block-target query. MainRenderLoop first updates free-mouse state and camera yaw/pitch; PlayerCamera updates at Before order 0, and the engine copies the perspective matrices immediately before Opaque. Do not use a game-tick-before-camera snapshot or the current Ortho matrix stack for world picking.
2. Window input coordinates and the inspected native viewport are in the same units: MouseX/MouseY are upper-left window coordinates; ClientMain.Width/Height and RenderAPIBase.FrameWidth/Height use Platform.WindowSize. Do not divide them by RuntimeEnv.GUIScale. For a resized/different native viewport, map by explicit viewport/window ratios, invalidate the old context and require a new successful draw before new capture. Zero-sized viewport or nonfinite/uninvertible matrices make the context unusable with feedback, not center-screen fallback.
3. Use the actual event X/Y on a free-cursor press, and the native integer Width/2, Height/2 reticle position when grabbed. Convert top-left Y to bottom-left Y for native projection math. Unproject near/far clip points with guarded Mat4d.Multiply/Invert/MulWithVec4 in doubles; check inversion result, finite homogeneous components, abs(W) > 1e-12 and direction length > 1e-12 before normalization. Unproject ignores the inversion result and PickingRayUtil checks only origin.X for NaN, so those native helpers are a reference for conventions, not sufficient validation by themselves. Use double viewport arithmetic for the inverse screen mapping; MatrixToolsd.Project's integer viewportWidth/2 and viewportHeight/2 require correction for odd dimensions rather than unqualified reuse.
4. Use the actual unprojected visual ray for both handle math and block following/creation, in both pointer modes. Keep its origin on that line (the unprojected near point, with normalized near-to-far direction); never translate it to the player eye. PickingRayUtil's eye-origin policy can displace an off-center cursor ray from the visible line in third person; Camera.GetCameraMatrix confirms the displaced camera. The player-angle RayTraceForSelection overload is not a generally equivalent reticle ray either. Native helpers qualify matrix conventions and filter behavior, but neither their origin policy nor player PickingRange is the chosen targeting policy. This deliberate deviation preserves the proposal's actual-pointer and user-confirmed far-plane targeting requirements.
5. For center dragging and first-use/missing-endpoint creation, pass Ray.FromPositions(unprojectedNear, unprojectedFar) to world.RayTraceForSelection with fresh local outputs. This is the finite visual segment through the camera far plane, not an arbitrary fixed length, eye-centered reach sphere or player PickingRange. The supplier overload uses the supplied Ray.Length and returns the first block/entity; retain foreground occlusion and do not filter nearer entities out to target a block behind them. Reproduce UpdateCurrentSelection's meta-block and interactable/nonself entity filters; retain current native liquid-selectability semantics without changing forceLiquidSelectable or player selection fields. Reject entity/missing targets, unloaded target chunks (GetChunkAtBlockPos returns null), and nonfinite or beyond-clip hit points before copying the selected cell/dimension; reconstruct the exact native hit with Position.InternalY plus HitPosition, then project it using the captured matrices, requiring finite components, W > 1e-12 and NDC Z in [-1,1] with 1e-6 tolerance for boundary roundoff. No terrain loads/generation, face-adjacent brush destinations or depth/GPU readbacks. Loaded-target qualification applies only to actual terrain picking, not stored endpoint validity or axis edits.
6. Native CurrentBlockSelection is refreshed by miw at Done order 0.9 and can remain stale with a free cursor when ordinary world interaction is not requested. Gizmo center updates therefore perform their own qualified selection, not a dependency on that late cache or a forced call to UpdateCurrentSelection (which invokes OnBeingLookedAt and changes native selection state).
7. Raw MouseMove API callbacks precede MouseCurrentX/Y, delta accumulation and camera processing. Observe event coordinates without handling movement; update drag/hover using the next perspective-frame context after native aiming. Button presses/releases use the last successfully drawn context for visual hits and terminal calculation; recompute their pointer target against that context, never a pending newer undrawn frame. Invalidate on viewport, world, dimension, cursor/camera-mode or eligibility changes. A release applies its final valid preview, then completes once; a stale/ineligible context cancels instead.
8. A native ray's Y is dimension-encoded at the world API boundary (EntityPos.XYZ uses InternalY). Reusable positions/rays used by gizmo math carry explicit dimension and local Y. Add/subtract dimension * BlockPos.DimensionBoundary in double precision only at native world/render boundaries; never infer an End anchor's dimension from its Y.

Later verification must assert projection/unprojection agreement at edges/center, portrait/wide/odd-sized viewports, GUI scales, first-/third-person camera offsets and dimension boundaries. For block targets cover a valid hit beyond ordinary player reach, the far-plane boundary and beyond it, nearer terrain/entity occlusion, unloaded targets and sky. Axis/handle interaction has no player-reach cap either; visible handle eligibility still obeys the camera clip interval and overlay hits do not raycast terrain for occlusion.

#### Native input ownership and cancellation contract

- Raw down/up update MouseStateRaw before native hotkey translation; raw-capturing systems may intercept first. The translated UpdateMouseButtonState invokes API MouseDown/Up before GUI/client dispatch and before setting/clearing InWorldMouseState. Do not assume GUI already handled the API event or that every physical release reaches it. Preserve native hotkey translation rather than replacing bindings; qualification establishes this supported native route, not arbitrary third-party raw-input interception compatibility.
- Before a new capture, reject previously handled events, lost focus, paused/dead/disabled/wrong-world contexts, any open modal dialog or CaptureAllInputs/CaptureRawMouse owner. For other native HUDs reserve their receiving composer bounds; for BuildModeHud use a side-effect-free exact interactive-cell/toggle hit predicate plus existing captures, not the entire panel or a stale hover-exit request. Let native GUI dispatch handle that event once. Passive size/Room/Controls text remains passive.
- Capture only fresh left down with a successfully drawn eligible handle, or the pending creation path below. Retain one instance/handle and baseline until release/cancel. Represent each owned left/right gesture as down-owned then release-retired; keep late LeftMouseDown/InWorldLeftMouseDown and RightMouseDown/InWorldRightMouseDown callbacks suppressed, before TryPlaceBlock, until the next fresh physical press retires that button's old latch. Ordinary nonowned presses pass through.
- Explicitly clear only owned InWorldMouseButton fields before consuming their releases/losing focus, because an API-handled MouseUp skips the engine's normal clear. Reconcile raw MouseButton for hidden releases; it is deliberately the obsolete raw state, not the routed world state. A hidden left release cancels rather than commits an uncertain final drag, while suppression retires safely.
- A right down during a drag or pending creation cancels/restores, owns the full right gesture and keeps any already owned left gesture suppressed through its release. On Escape during either operation, cancel once and consume its down/repeat/up lifecycle. API KeyDown precedes global hotkeys and GUI Escape dispatch; without an owned operation, leave normal Escape/dialog behavior alone. If a dialog has already taken priority, cancel the edit without stealing that dialog's key gesture.
- Never handle reticle MouseMove, set camera angles, force MouseGrabbed, or set mouseWorldInteractAnyway. Native UpdateCameraYawPitch requires focus, AllowCameraControl and grabbed mouse; its normal movement must continue during reticle capture. Free-cursor movement does not enable look.
- Check eligibility before every button event/frame update, not only the 50-ms brush tick. Dialog opening, focus loss, cursor/camera-mode switch, mode exit, death, dimension/session change and hiding cancel first; then retire capture and retry any earlier committed dirty save only at the existing durable boundary. The native focus list fires before clearing raw/world mouse state and must be explicitly detached. HUD collapse/recomposition/locale/GUI-scale rebuild alone does not cancel world captures; a viewport change invalidates/reprojects the visual context without inventing endpoint changes.

#### Reusable instance/value/manager contract

- Gizmo : IDisposable owns identity, visible/enabled state, hover/captured handle, render/hit hooks and begin/update/complete/cancel lifecycle. One client-session manager owns membership, deterministic arbitration and exclusive capture; session adapters alone subscribe/register native events/render stages. No ruler, grid, placement, config or entity dependencies in the base.
- Base handle IDs are opaque and priorities are supplied by each gizmo; only PositionGizmo defines Center, X, Y, Z. Use stable manager insertion IDs for ties. Reject hidden/disabled/disposed/failed/wrong-dimension/behind-camera targets before arbitration. Within a positioning instance prefer a hit center over its arrow roots; otherwise choose minimum positive visual-ray hit distance, then Center/X/Y/Z order for exact ties. Across instances sort candidates by floor(hit distance / 1e-6), then insertion ID, yielding a transitive deterministic depth key rather than a pairwise epsilon comparator. Do not change candidate midcapture or add ruler selection controls.
- Gizmo<TValue> accepts an initial value and exposes Value plus Action<TValue> OnValueChanged. EqualityComparer<TValue>.Default compares before storing; store before invoking the event. Differing owner assignments and user updates both notify synchronously once; identical snapped values and identical owner echoes do not. No SetValue, silent update path or notification for internal mutation of an unchanged reference. Use immutable values for positioning.
- Begin records the generic baseline before owner begin notification. Every accepted drag update uses Value. Complete ends capture and issues one completion notification (no extra equal-value event); the owner decides changed commit versus dirty retry. Cancel restores Value first if changed, then issues cancellation, never completion. Release the manager's capture and ensure one terminal notification even when an owner removes/disposes the instance during notification.
- The ruler binding handles ordinary Value echoes as no-ops. For a genuinely different external authoritative replacement during capture: cancel/restore the original transaction first, then assign through Value and synchronize the controller outside preview handling. A cancellation must never restore over the incoming value. Preview value events are not config-save events.
- Disposal is idempotent and cancels/restores before detaching membership/subscriptions/resources; queued work checks session identity and disposal. Invalid targets/geometry are rejected with reasoned feedback and last valid value; invalid owner arguments are explicit contract errors, not success-shaped defaults. Native input/render failure is logged/notified, cancels and disables affected hit targets without disabling size/config functionality.

#### Position, snapping and axis numerical contract

- GizmoPosition is an immutable value of double X/Y/Z plus int Dimension, with exact component equality and finite coordinates. No implicit block snapping. Ruler conversion uses offsets Start=(0,0,0), End=(1,1,1) without role swapping. Do arithmetic in double/wide integer before any checked int conversion; validate decoded cells with RulerEndpoint.IsValid (local Y in [0,32768), nonnegative encodable dimension and accessor.IsValidPos).
- For an axis, choose a fixed axis-containing drag plane at begin. Let p0 be baseline anchor, a its unit axis, d0 the initial normalized visual ray direction; n = normalize(d0 - a*dot(d0,a)). Reject begin if its squared pre-normalization length <= 1e-4. Keep p0/a/n fixed until terminal interaction.
- Intersect each current ray o+t*d with that plane: t=dot(p0-o,n)/dot(d,n). Reject abs(dot(d,n)) <= 1e-2, t < 0, nonfinite results, or t > 10000*max(1,initial camera-to-anchor distance). Let s=dot(o+t*d-p0,a); preserve grab offset by using displacement s-s0, not absolute s. Hold the other two components exactly at p0.
- As an additional huge-jump guard, reject an axis update differing from the last accepted axis coordinate by more than max(64,8*initial camera-to-anchor distance) blocks. Keep the last accepted value and surface a degeneration/invalid-update notice rather than clamping or silently switching axes/planes. These are explicit initial safety thresholds for Phase 3 boundary tests; tune only with recorded numerical/live evidence, not by removing finite/jump guards.
- Generic motion publishes the accepted continuous anchor. The ruler rounds displacement to whole blocks with MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero before adding it to the baseline axis coordinate; never truncate absolute negative coordinates. Examples: +/-0.49 -> 0; +0.5 -> +1; -0.5 -> -1. Reject invalid decoded cells/dimension changes before publishing; center resolution takes the actual selected cell and reapplies that role's offset with no plane fallback.
- At begin snapshot both ruler endpoints. Valid value events update the selected endpoint/size/box live and preserve the other. Completion keeps the final preview and issues one changed commit; cancellation restores the pair with no preview save. A no-op explicit completion can retry an earlier dirty commit. Departure cancels a preview before persistence.Dispose can read controller state; existing unreadable/unsupported-document protection and per-world schema stay intact.

#### Creation, rendering and provisional visual contract

- Show ruler on with neither endpoint arms creation only while mode/alive/session eligibility holds. With a same-dimension partial ruler, offer the missing-endpoint creation gesture under the same visibility/eligibility conditions. Hit an existing eligible handle first; only a miss goes to pending creation. That preserves the existing partial handle's editability. An ordinary miss without pending creation passes through.
- One valid fresh left creation press creates both empty endpoints on that cell, or only the missing endpoint of a partial ruler; commit once and own the full gesture without a simultaneous drag. Invalid block/entity/sky/missing targets consume the attempted creation gesture, notify and stay pending for a fresh press.
- Right/Escape explicitly cancel pending creation until the next off/on Show ruler request or explicit build-mode re-enable. Hiding, mode/death/focus/dialog/cursor/dimension/session loss retires it; absent an explicit cancellation latch, a remaining empty/partial state may be offered again on the next eligible context, never while a mouse button is already held. A retained endpoint in another dimension prevents creation/automatic relocation there; preserve size/state, hide handles and use a dimension-mismatch notice. No new key, button or selection state.
- Keep box rendering at its existing Opaque 0.51 stage. Use one session gizmo render adapter registered at Opaque and AfterBlit, order 0.49, unregistered from both identically. Opaque refreshes the perspective context/drag preview before the box draw; AfterBlit draws overlay geometry on the default framebuffer before Ortho UI, with depth test/write off, saved matrices and opaque colors (no blend-state mutation). An eligible pending-creation session also needs these context updates with zero instances: publish the valid context without uploading/drawing a handle or inventing a hit target. Handles remain pickable through terrain but not through explicit GUI ownership.
- Reuse unit line-arrow meshes/native Wireframe shader and line-state snapshot discipline; propose a 16-segment Start circle and End diamond plus RGB arrows, with enlarged geometry/line emphasis for hover/active cues. Share meshes, transform per instance; no per-frame upload/readback/entity/tessellation. Balanced shader/depth/line restoration applies on failure; if blending is ever changed, extend snapshot/restore first.
- Initial visual targets in window pixels: arrow length 64, center radius 7, arrow-head length 12, pick padding 4; scale these once by GUI scale. Determine world scale from the captured projection's pixels-per-world-unit and clamp axis length to [0.125,8] blocks. Hit geometry uses the resulting actually rendered dimensions plus the pixel padding, not the unclamped requested size. Reject anchors with clip W <= 1e-12 or outside the near/far clip interval; overlay means terrain-independent, not behind-camera picking.
- The manager uses the last successfully drawn eligible context for new hits; hidden/failed/unrendered instances have no invisible hit targets. Render failure cancels active edits and logs/notifies. Queue every renderer membership change requested inside a render callback, not just failure retirement, outside native live-list iteration (including extended-debug profiling); eligibility/hit rejection takes effect immediately. One session teardown cancels before detaching subscriptions and disposing shared GPU resources; late tasks must not touch the next world.

Phase 1 evidence boundary: the source-backed native paths and deterministic contracts above satisfy qualification/definition work only. Phases 2-6 must implement and test them; Phase 7 must qualify both pointer modes, camera behavior, readability/GUI scales, overlaps, terrain/transparent materials, native action suppression and restart persistence in the supported game. No live result or old selection-bug diagnosis is claimed here.

## Phase 2: Implement reusable gizmo value and lifecycle foundations

Prerequisites: Phase 1 contracts.
Outcome: A test gizmo can publish values and participate in manager arbitration without ruler or global gameplay dependencies.

    ✔ Add abstract Gizmo : IDisposable with the proposal's shared instance responsibilities.
        ✔ Encapsulate identity, visible/enabled state, hovered/captured handle and lifecycle notifications.
        ✔ Expose rendering/hit-testing and interaction hooks through shared camera/world context; add an interface only for a demonstrated consumer need.
        ✔ Keep ruler cells, grid policy, brush placement and config side effects out of the base.
    ✔ Add Gizmo<TValue> with initial value, public Value getter/setter and OnValueChanged.
        ✔ Compare before storing/notifying; changed owner and manipulation assignments use the same event path.
        ✔ Suppress equal assignments, snapped duplicates and identical callback echoes; do not add SetValue or a silent synchronization escape hatch.
        ✔ Restore the initial drag value on cancellation with a change notification only when needed; never signal a successful commit for cancellation.
        ✔ Support owner-coordinated cancel-before-external-replacement without applying rollback after the replacement.
        ✔ Document immutable-value expectations rather than claiming to detect mutation inside arbitrary reference values.
    ✔ Add a small client-owned manager for instance membership and arbitration.
        ✔ Select one deterministic hit, prioritize a center over its own arrow roots, and grant at most one capture.
        ✔ Retain the captured gizmo/handle until completion or cancellation, including pointer movement outside the silhouette.
        ✔ Hidden/disabled/removed/disposed instances lose eligibility and active capture safely; repeated removal/disposal is harmless.
        ✔ Keep global input/render event registration in adapters/session composition, not per-instance or per-frame registration.
    ✔ Add focused base/typed/manager tests using a minimal test gizmo.
        ✔ Assert event values/count/order for owner edits, manipulation, equal echoes, cancellation and external replacement.
        ✔ Assert deterministic hit/capture ownership and disposal without ruler/config/world mutations.

Completion criteria:

    ✔ Typed property events and lifecycle/manager behavior pass focused tests without ruler-specific dependencies.
    ✔ No new global gameplay adapter is active yet; the existing ruler is not partially replaced.

### Phase 2 implementation traceability

All groups inherit the complete proposal and Phase 1 reusable instance/value/manager
contract. Native input, projection/positioning geometry, ruler transactions and cutover
remain in their assigned later phases; the existing ruler is unchanged.

