### Phase 3.1: Evaluate camera-relative float matrix transformations

Prerequisites: Completed Phase 3 implementation.
Outcome: An evidence-backed decision on using built-in System.Numerics.Matrix4x4/Vector4
for camera-relative transformations instead of the current double-precision matrix path.
This follow-up does not invalidate the completed Phase 3 implementation
or require migration merely to complete the evaluation.

Controlling sources: [proposal](./InWorldGizmos.proposal.md), especially positioning,
dragging, rendering and verification; Phase 1 pointer/numerical contracts;
[camera transformations](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/GizmoCamera.cs);
[geometry/picking](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/PositionGizmoGeometry.cs);
and [render transformations](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/GizmoRenderAdapter.cs).

    ☑ Define the candidate precision boundary and comparison criteria before evaluating.
        ☑ Keep absolute world positions, dimension conversion and camera-reference subtraction in double precision; convert only camera-relative inputs to float.
        ☑ Identify which projection, inverse/unprojection and final render transformations can use Matrix4x4/Vector4, accounting for native column-vector versus System.Numerics row-vector conventions and multiplication order.
        ☑ Set explicit screen-space, ray/target-distance and manipulation-error budgets for the tested near/far ranges; retain existing coordinate validity, dimension identity, snapping and unchanged-component guarantees.
    ☑ Compare a bounded candidate against the current double implementation using existing test infrastructure.
        ☑ Cover free cursor and reticle, first-/third-person offsets, odd/portrait/wide viewports and GUI scales.
        ☑ Cover large positive/negative world coordinates with small relative offsets, nonzero dimensions and boundary-local Y.
        ☑ Measure projection/unprojection round trips, inverse conditioning, near/far clip classification and finite far-point reconstruction, including targets beyond player reach.
        ☑ Compare visible handle geometry, hit ownership/tie ordering, grab offsets, axis degeneracy guards and snapped endpoint results, including near decision boundaries.
        ☑ Record failing cases and worst observed errors rather than relaxing existing contracts to make the candidate pass.
    ☑ Evaluate performance and implementation cost before recommending adoption.
        ☑ Use existing benchmark facilities to compare representative repeated transforms and camera-snapshot inversion/setup, accounting for conversion costs and allocations.
        ☑ Record runtime, hardware/SIMD support, workload and measured results; do not infer a speedup from API choice alone.
        ☑ Compare a rendering-only float path with broader projection/unprojection use if the latter fails precision requirements.
    ☑ Record the decision and concise evidence in this plan.
        ☑ Recommend retaining doubles, adopting float only at a safe boundary, or a separately scoped migration, with supported limitations and affected consumers.
        ☑ Keep production behavior unchanged during evaluation; remove temporary prototype code unless retained as a justified test/benchmark fixture.
        ☑ Do not treat synthetic precision/performance results as live-game qualification or silently introduce a precision fallback.

Completion criteria:

    ☑ Correctness/error budgets and representative performance results support a documented decision, even if the candidate is rejected.
    ☑ Any recommended migration has explicit scope and regression obligations; evaluation completion alone does not authorize or claim that migration.
    ☑ Second review and independent completion audit confirm the evaluation evidence and decision satisfy this subphase.

#### Phase 3.1 evaluation criteria (set before execution)

The evaluated candidate used System.Numerics.Matrix4x4/Vector4 only after double
camera-reference subtraction, retaining double absolute positions and explicit dimensions.
Native column-major matrices map to transposed row-vector matrices; composition becomes
view * projection, and a model-view transform becomes model * view. No native projection
builder is replaced (the existing OpenGL depth convention must remain intact).

Acceptance budgets for this evaluation: maximum projected/round-trip/handle-vertex error
0.25 window pixels; near/far/target reconstruction and ray-intersection error 0.001 blocks;
unsnapped axis/grab-offset error 0.0001 blocks; matrix/inverse identity residual 0.00001.
All outputs must be finite. Existing clip tolerance (NDC 0.000001), degeneracy/jump guards,
dimension identity, selected handle/tie ordering, snapped cell results and untouched axis
components must not change in the tested cases, including threshold-adjacent probes.
Measure near=0.1 with far=128/1000/10000, varied pointer/viewport/GUI scale/camera offsets,
large signed world references and nonzero-dimension local-Y boundaries. Budget failures
reject that candidate scope, not the evaluation. A narrower render-only candidate retains
double picking/inversion/drag math and compares final GPU matrix/vertex inputs separately.

#### Phase 3.1 evaluation evidence and decision

Historical evaluation traceability (the controlling sources above apply to each group):
- Precision boundary -> proposal positioning/rendering, Phase 1 numerical contracts,
  GizmoCamera -> the test-only candidate retained double reference subtraction,
  explicit dimensions and the native projection; transposed matrix conventions before
  Matrix4x4 composition/inversion. No production source changed.
- Correctness -> proposal verification, Phase 1 pointer/drag contracts, GizmoCamera,
  PositionGizmoGeometry and PositionGizmo -> evaluation probes compared the actual double camera, shared geometry,
  manager hit/tie arbitration and drag implementation against float-derived inputs.
- Rendering/performance -> proposal rendering, GizmoRenderAdapter.ModelView ->
  rendering probes compared the same parts through native-compatible float GPU arrays;
  microbenchmarks measured transforms, setup/inversion and final model-view construction.
  After evaluation, the user rejected adoption and requested removal of all float
  prototypes, including the rendering-only variant, evaluation test and benchmark
  switch. They have been removed; the measurements below are historical evidence,
  not a retained runnable evaluation. Production double math remains unchanged.

Coverage: 108 configurations, 432 pointer samples, 1296 targets, 11016 handle/render
vertices each, 648 hit probes, 7128 axis probes and 648 clip probes. Viewport/scale
pairs are 801x601/1, 400x900/0.75 and 1920x500/2, in free-cursor/reticle and
first-/third-person modes (third-person relative offset -3,-2,-4). Near=0.1;
far=128/1000/10000. References include fractional positive/negative X/Z near
2 billion, dimensions 0/2/15, and local Y 32768/-0.25. Targets cover 0.25, 5
and 75% of far distance; rendered geometry covers 0.25, 10 and 75% of far.
Actual synthetic target-plane intersections supplement same-distance ray comparisons.
Axis probes include a 0.75 grab offset, all axes, half-block snapping boundaries,
and begin/update degeneracy thresholds; coincident circle/diamond handles test ties.

Observed maximum errors (not relaxed to obtain acceptance):
- Projection/handle vertices 0.000932343 pixels; round trip 0.006014792 pixels.
- Near reconstruction 0.000000719354 blocks; far reconstruction 55.4342803 blocks.
- Target and target-plane intersection 0.0152320475 blocks.
- Unsnapped axis displacement 0.000001907349 blocks; geometry axis-length
  difference 0.00118177816 blocks (diagnostic, not a separate acceptance budget).
- Inverse identity residual 0.00000572508; infinity-norm condition estimate 831.828493.
- No nonfinite outputs, axis-acceptance differences, dimension differences or
  untouched-component differences. However: 20 clip differences, 6 hit differences
  and 746 snapped-delta differences. Broad candidate FAILS the existing contracts.
- Rendering-only candidate: maximum 0.001102686 pixels, GPU model-view coefficient
  difference 0.000161171, no clip differences/nonfinite matrices; PASSES the tested
  screen-space budget. Matrix coefficient error is diagnostic, not an inverse residual.

Representative failures: far reconstruction peaks at 1920x500/scale2, free cursor,
first person, far10000, dimension2, reference (2000000000.125,32768,-2000000000.25),
pointer (1919,499). A far128 third-person clip probe at
(3.1611615431964957,5.25,3.4067672654393966), dimension0, is accepted only by doubles.
At 801x601/scale1, dimension0, pointer (411.50010000000009,300.5), the same instance
selects double handle3 versus float handle0. A requested X delta0.5 becomes
0.49999999219708213 in the float-ray path versus 0.5 in doubles, changing AwayFromZero
snapping. Small screen error therefore does not establish safe world-space picking.

Final local microbenchmark: Release, .NET 10.0.14, Windows 10.0.26200, X64,
Intel64 Family 6 Model151 Stepping2 GenuineIntel; Vector128/Vector256/Vector4 hardware
support true. DOTNET_TieredCompilation=0; 50000 warmup iterations and seven trials;
500000 transform, 10000 setup and 100000 model-view iterations per trial. Transform
inputs cycle 128 values, model origins vary, and checksums consume results.

Operation                                      Median ns/op    Range ns/op        B/op
Double transform                                   6.413       5.113-11.380          0
Double transform + reference subtraction            5.685       4.638-6.872           0
Float transform, preconverted                       2.689       1.967-5.494           0
Float transform + conversion                        3.971       3.043-7.030           0
Double camera setup                             4185.470    2423.280-5265.080       904
Float conversion/inversion kernel                2930.610    1516.630-6078.480       336
Double render model-view + GPU array              252.053     218.593-414.859        544
Float render model-view + conversion/GPU array     267.296     153.069-399.351         88

The float setup kernel borrows existing immutable camera metadata: this is not an
end-to-end camera replacement measurement. Timings vary substantially on this shared
host; the final render median is not faster despite fewer allocations. Neither API
choice nor these noisy microbenchmarks establish a frame-time/FPS improvement.

Decision: retain double camera projection/inversion, pointer rays, position values,
dimension handling, geometry sizing/hits and drag/snapping. Reject the evaluated
broad float pipeline, without claiming every relative-float design is impossible.
The rendering-only variant met the tested visual budget but is also declined and
removed at the user's request. No float migration is planned or authorized. Any future
reconsideration requires a new scoped evaluation and numerical/native-render regressions,
with native order/GPU output, state/error handling and retirement preserved. This
evaluation provides no live-game evidence.

Pre-removal verification subagent: 259 passed, zero failed/skipped; .\build.ps1 --target=Build
passed including ValidateJson; git diff --check passed. Existing nullable warnings
and CakeBuild System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs 10.0.12 advisories remain unrelated.
Direct second review completed, including the expanded intersection/dimension/far
render probes. Fresh independent read-only completion audit inspected the controlling
sources and current implementation, reused applicable verification and found the phase
fully satisfied with no required findings. Phase 3.1 is complete; later phases unchanged.
Post-removal verification: 258 remaining Gizmos/Ruler tests passed, zero failed/skipped;
no float-evaluation source references remain and git diff --check passed.

## Phase 4: Implement native input capture for both pointer modes

Prerequisites: Phases 1-3.
Outcome: The manager can receive native-adapter gestures safely in a test composition, ready for a single-owner gameplay cutover.

    ☑ Implement one native input adapter for manager interactions.
        ☑ Respect earlier handled input, open/capturing dialogs and actual HUD hits before new gizmo capture.
        ☑ Use free-cursor rays without camera look and reticle rays while retaining camera look needed for aiming.
        ☑ Begin only on a fresh left press over an eligible handle; do not change targets during capture.
        ☑ Leave presses outside handles on the normal path except explicitly pending creation.
    ☑ Implement full-gesture suppression and repeatability.
        ☑ Suppress captured hold/repeat/release and late native world/control action callbacks before brush placement.
        ☑ Release completes once; right-click and Escape cancel/restore without placement, item use or unintended dialog activation.
        ☑ Own the right-cancel gesture and safely retire latches without blocking the next fresh edit.
        ☑ Handle hidden releases and focus changes using the qualified native ordering, not assumed callback delivery.
    ☑ Cancel interaction on dialog opening, focus loss, pointer-mode change, build-mode exit, death, dimension change and departure.
        ☑ Cancel before losing the context needed to restore preview state.
        ☑ Do not treat unrelated HUD recomposition as a world-gizmo cancellation.
    ☑ Test the actual adapter/manager route with native carriers and service doubles where appropriate.
        ☑ Cover both pointer modes, GUI/handled priority, drag outside handles, cancellation and hidden/late releases.
        ☑ Assert no modeled mining/attack/use/placement callbacks or packets for owned gestures and intact ordinary input otherwise.
        ☑ Repeat completed and canceled captures many times; verify no stale latches, duplicate callbacks or registrations.
        ☑ Keep native engine ordering evidence separate from modeled downstream suppression evidence.

Completion criteria:

    ☑ Both pointer modes and complete gesture retirement pass focused adapter/manager tests.
    ☑ Adapter activation in normal gameplay remains gated on Phase 6 cutover; old/new ruler adapters never compete.

### Phase 4 implementation and evidence

Controlling sources: complete proposal, especially input/dragging, visibility/lifecycle
and verification; Phase 1 native input ownership/pointer/creation contracts above;
the linked RulerInput, BuildBrushSystem_Client, BuildModeHud and architecture context.
Native event/focus ordering remains the installed 1.22.7 source qualification recorded
in Phase 1, not a new live compatibility claim.

Traceability:
- Native route/priority -> proposal activation and Phase 1 ownership ->
  GizmoInputAdapter subscribes once per explicitly constructed session to mouse,
  Escape, dimension, departure and native focus callbacks, and renderer ContextReady.
  Its owner must route HandleWorldAction before brush placement and call Refresh
  when eligibility changes even if rendering is inactive. No production composition
  constructs the adapter; Phase 6 retains atomic old/new ownership cutover.
- Actual pointers/capture -> Phase 1 perspective-frame contract -> free press/release
  uses event coordinates and the last successfully drawn context; Opaque updates use
  the new camera after native aiming. MouseMove only records coordinates, never handles
  movement or changes camera/cursor settings. Manager retains the captured handle.
- GUI reservation -> Phase 1 exact HUD contract -> BuildModeHud.ReservesPointer and
  GuiElementTable.ReservesPointer test real controls/captures without stale hover-exit
  routing or passive panel bounds; other receiving native HUD composers reserve bounds.
  Modal/raw/all-input dialogs prevent capture and cancel previews without stealing keys.
- Full gesture/termination -> proposal no gameplay side effects and Phase 1 latches ->
  per-button Owned/Retired states suppress both native action families through late
  callbacks until a fresh physical press; owned world-button fields are explicitly
  cleared. Hidden releases cancel, visible valid releases apply the final pointer then
  complete once. Right/Escape cancel; focus/context loss rolls back before retirement.
  Raw KeyboardKeyStateRaw (IInputAPI) also retires a hidden Escape release outside
  key callbacks, without depending on raw-key update order inside KeyDown/KeyUp.
- Lifecycle/failure -> proposal session ownership -> eligibility is checked on buttons,
  keys, world actions and frames, including mode/death/world/dimension, pointer/camera
  mode and viewport. Disposal cancels before detaching all subscriptions; recognized
  input failures log/notify once and disable input for the session. HUD rebuilds alone
  do not invalidate world capture.
- Pending-creation seam -> Phase 1 handle-before-creation policy ->
  IGizmoCreationInput receives only a miss from a fresh owned press, including sessions
  with no instances; cancellation distinguishes explicit Right/Escape from context
  retirement so the future owner can preserve its rearming policy. Actual terrain resolution, ruler creation and durable transactions
  remain Phases 5/6; the adapter owns cancellation and suppression, not ruler state.

Focused evidence: GizmoInputAdapterTests uses the actual manager, PositionGizmo and
two-stage renderer with native ClientPlayer/EntityPlayer carriers and API service doubles.
It covers free/reticle and first-/third-person pointers, beyond-reach handles, priority,
final release, out-of-bounds drag, hidden releases, cancellation/rollback, late actions,
held-at-construction, focus reset, creation, failures, teardown and 120 complete/cancel
cycles per pointer mode. BuildModeHudControlsTests adds actual composed control/passive/
native-HUD routing and stale-hover/recomposition checks. Downstream mining/attack/use/
placement/packet gates are modeled, not a live engine or multiplayer execution proof.
Verification subagent: 390 focused tests passed, zero failed/skipped, using Release
and filter Gizmos|Ruler|BuildModeHudControlsTests|TableInputTests. Normal
